{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Because of his young age a regency was established led by his mother and later by his uncle Joscelin II. During his minority the county faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal instability. Raymond III was eventually knighted and assumed full control of his lands in 1157. His early reign was marked by efforts to restore order and rebuild the defenses of Tripoli which had suffered from years of neglect and conflict.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a cousin of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 and again in 1183. His leadership was controversial because he often favored diplomacy over warfare. He was accused of being too cautious and even of treason by some of his rivals. Raymond opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and the military orders. He believed that alliances with Muslim powers could secure peace and stability. His diplomatic efforts included negotiations with Nur ad-Din and later with Saladin. Though these efforts were seen as weak by many crusaders they reflected his deep understanding of the region’s complex realities.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He refused to join Guy of Lusignan in launching a reckless campaign against Saladin. Raymond argued that the crusader army was not ready and that they should avoid battle until better prepared. His warnings were ignored. After the disastrous defeat at Hattin Raymond 